    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.assortativity_nominal - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.assortativity_nominal.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.assortativity_nominal()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Calculate assortativity for categorical (nominal) node attributes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.assortativity_nominal()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Calculate assortativity for categorical (nominal) node attributes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.assortativity_nominal()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Calculate assortativity for categorical (nominal) node attributes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCalculate assortativity for categorical (nominal) node attributes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L83480-L83592){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.assortativity_nominal(attribute)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Nominal assortativity](../assets/graphs/assortativity-nominal.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Two triangles joined by one edge, with the nodes split into two categories: A, B, C in the accent color and D, E, F in teal. Six of the seven edges join nodes of the same category, so mixing is assortative and r = 0.714.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nMeasures the tendency of nodes with the same attribute value to connect, also known as\nhomophily. Uses Newman's formula for categorical mixing patterns: it compares the fraction\nof edges that join nodes of the same category with the fraction you would expect if edges\nwere placed at random given how many edge ends each category has.\n\nA single number summarizing \"do like nodes link to like nodes?\" is useful for:\n\n- measuring homophily in social networks (by department, school, party, language)\n- checking whether communities or clusters line up with a known label\n- detecting disassortative structure, such as buyer-seller or host-parasite networks,\n  where connections mostly cross categories\n\nAttribute values are compared by their string form, so `1` and `\"1\"` count as the same\ncategory. Every edge is counted, including self-loops and parallel edges. For undirected\ngraphs each edge counts in both directions; for directed graphs the mixing matrix is\nbuilt from source-to-target pairs only. A consequence is that in a directed graph where\nevery edge runs from one category to another (for example, predator to prey) the\nexpected same-category fraction is already zero, so the coefficient is `0` rather than\nnegative.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[attribute]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Name of the categorical node attribute to analyze, such as `\"group\"` or `\"country\"`.\n    Every node in the graph must have this attribute, and at least two distinct values\n    must occur. Values of any type are accepted (they are compared as strings).\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Assortativity coefficient, at most `1`:\n\n    - `r = 1`: perfect assortativity (only same-type connections)\n    - `r > 0`: assortative (same types prefer to connect)\n    - `r = 0`: random mixing\n    - `r < 0`: disassortative (different types prefer to connect)\n\n    The minimum value depends on the category proportions; `-1` is reached only in\n    special cases such as a perfectly bipartite undirected graph with two equally sized\n    (by edge ends) categories.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph has no edges, the attribute is missing on any node, there are fewer than\n    two different attribute values, or the edges give no basis for comparison (for\n    example, every edge end belongs to a single category).\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nFor a categorical attribute this is Newman's *modularity by attribute*:\n$r = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{tr}(E) - \\|E^2\\|_1}{1 - \\|E^2\\|_1}$, where $E$ is the mixing\nmatrix whose entries $e_{ij}$ record the fraction of edges running between\nattribute values $i$ and $j$. $r = 1$ means the graph is fully segregated by\nattribute (only within-class edges); $r = 0$ matches random mixing at the given\nmarginals; negative $r$ signals systematic cross-class connections. This is the\ntool that formalizes the \"birds of a feather\" intuition, and negative variants\nsurface bipartite-style structures like predator-prey webs or user-item\nrecommendation graphs. Building the mixing matrix is a single $O(V + E)$ pass.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~attribute_mixing_matrix`) \n\n:   The matrix the coefficient is computed from.\n\n[](`~assortativity`) \n\n:   The coefficient for numeric attributes.\n\n[](`~modularity`) \n\n:   How much more often links stay within groups than at random.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nTwo of the three links stay within a team, but random links between nodes with these\ndegrees would keep half within one, so the coefficient is only `1/3`:\n\n::: {#3cf7ff1e .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\")])\ngraph = graph.set_node_attrs(\n    \"team\", {\"A\": \"red\", \"B\": \"red\", \"C\": \"blue\", \"D\": \"blue\"}\n)\n\nround(graph.assortativity_nominal(\"team\"), 3)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n0.333\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Clubs, not ages\n\n`friends()` links 24 members of two clubs in a made-up town, a chess club and a climbing\nclub, who are friends. People are friends with people from their own club far more than\nrandom friendships would make them, but not with people their own age:\n\n::: {#1309ecb8 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\n\n{\n    attr: round(friends.assortativity_nominal(attr), 3)\n    for attr in [\"club\", \"age_group\"]\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n{'club': 0.767, 'age_group': 0.005}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nColored by age group, most friendships join people of different ages:\n\n::: {#f20405f1 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends.plot(\n    color_by=\"age_group\", edge_color_by=\"age_group\",\n    label_by=\"label\", seed=8,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-8b5e09c7:#52525b;--ect-61169ea5:#